    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Actually, I was looking for more of a technical breakdown for a high school curriculum. Could you skip the metaphors and focus specifically on the biochemical mechanism? I need to know the exact steps of the enzymatic breakdown of the amino acid sulfoxides. How does the alliinase facilitate the conversion specifically?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly, let's dive into the biochemical specifics. When you cut an onion, the cell walls are disrupted, and the enzyme alliinase is released into the surrounding tissue. Alliinase is a thioesterase enzyme that specifically targets the amino acid sulfoxides, such as S-allyl-L-cysteine sulfoxide, which are abundant in onions.\n\nThe reaction catalyzed by alliinase involves the cleavage of the S-allyl group from the sulfoxide, resulting in the formation of propanethial S-oxide and S-allyl-L-cysteine. The reaction can be represented as follows:\n\nS-allyl-L-cysteine sulfoxide + H2O → Propanethial S-oxide + S-allyl-L-cysteine\n\nThe alliinase enzyme facilitates this reaction by stabilizing the transition state and lowering the activation energy required for the reaction to proceed. The enzyme's active site binds to the S-allyl-L-cysteine sulfoxide, positioning the substrate in such a way that the S-allyl group is cleaved off, and the propanethial S-oxide is formed.\n\nPropanethial S-oxide is a volatile compound",
      "source": "target",
